    {
      "lesson_num": 1,
      "section_type": "Reading",
      "title": "Endangered Animals",
      "content": "Today, there are some endangered animals on Earth. It means that we can find only a few of them around us. Some examples are Iranian cheetahs, pandas, and blue whales. Humans destroy the natural homes of the animals in the forests, lakes, and plains. When the number of people on Earth increases, they need more places for living. They cut down trees and destroy lakes. They make homes and roads instead. Then the animals won't have a place to live. They will die out. Also, hunters hunt them for their meat or skins. If we take care of them, their number will increase. We can protect their natural homes and teach people how to love nature."
    },
    {
      "lesson_num": 1,
      "section_type": "Conversation",
      "title": "Saving Nature Conversation",
      "content": "Maryam: Look at this cheetah. Is it an endangered animal?\nTeacher: Yes, it is. The Iranian cheetah is in danger of dying out.\nMaryam: Why? What is the problem?\nTeacher: Humans destroy their homes and hunt them. Also, they build roads through their natural plains.\nMaryam: How can we save them?\nTeacher: We can protect their habitats and stop cutting down trees."
    },
    {
      "lesson_num": 2,
      "section_type": "Reading",
      "title": "A Wonder of Creation: The Human Heart",
      "content": "The human body is truly a wonder of creation. The heart is one of the most powerful organs in the body. It pumps blood continuously through thousands of kilometers of blood vessels. Blood carries red and white cells. Red blood cells carry oxygen and nutrients to all parts of the body. White blood cells are like brave soldiers that defend our body against dangerous microbes, viruses, and illnesses. When you run, jump, or exercise, your active muscles need more oxygen and energy. So, your heart beats much faster to deliver oxygen quickly. Eating healthy food and regular exercise keep our heart strong."
    },
    {
      "lesson_num": 2,
      "section_type": "Conversation",
      "title": "Astronomy and Planets",
      "content": "Ali: Look at the sky through this powerful telescope!\nSina: What can you see?\nAli: I can see Saturn and its wonderful rings.\nSina: That is amazing! Our solar system is full of wonders.\nAli: Yes, but Earth is the most wonderful planet because it has water and oxygen for living things. We should thank Allah for this great creation."
    },
    {
      "lesson_num": 3,
      "section_type": "Reading",
      "title": "Dr. Mohammad Gharib: The Dedicated Physician",
      "content": "Dr. Mohammad Gharib was born in Tehran in 1909. He was known as the father of modern pediatrics in Iran. After studying medicine in France, he returned home to help his people. He was a generous and compassionate physician who loved children. He treated sick children and never accepted money from poor patients. He founded the first specialized children's hospital in Tehran and trained hundreds of medical students. Great scientists like Dr. Gharib and Louis Pasteur teach us that true success comes from patience, hard work, and serving humanity."
    },
    {
      "lesson_num": 4,
      "section_type": "Reading",
      "title": "Iran: A Wonderful Destination",
      "content": "Every year, millions of tourists and pilgrims travel to Iran. Iran is an ancient country with rich history, beautiful architecture, and hospitable people. Cities like Isfahan and Shiraz are world-famous tourist destinations. In Shiraz, tourists can visit Persepolis, beautiful Persian gardens, and the tombs of great poets like Hafez and Saadi. In Isfahan, visitors admire blue-tiled mosques, Naqsh-e Jahan Square, and ancient bridges like Si-o-se-pol. Tourists can also buy delicious local sweets and handmade handicrafts in traditional bazaars."
    }
  ],
